WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.817 --> 00:00:09.083 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to the North America capitals, episode four of five. First, I clear
the chosen names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09.739 --> 00:00:20.060 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Five capitals of the smaller islands: the Bahamas, then the Leeward chain of
Saint Kitts, Antigua, Dominica and Saint Lucia.

3
00:00:20.729 --> 00:00:22.471 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Nassau into view.

4
00:00:26.451 --> 00:00:33.870 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Nassau stands on the harbour of New Providence, the small island where most of
the Bahamas' people live.

5
00:00:34.529 --> 00:00:45.872 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Named in 1695 for William III, of the house of Orange-Nassau, it was a
pirates' republic early in the eighteenth century, and Blackbeard lived here.

6
00:00:46.523 --> 00:00:52.618 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Nassau as the pirates' republic of New Providence, named for William
of Orange.

7
00:00:53.301 --> 00:00:55.902 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Basseterre into view.

8
00:00:59.869 --> 00:01:06.696 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Basseterre stands on the Caribbean side of Saint Kitts, in a lowland ringed by
the island's volcanic hills.

9
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:24.532 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
French for "low land", and founded by the French in 1627; it is one of the
oldest towns of the eastern Caribbean. Its Circus, a small roundabout with a
clock, was modelled on Piccadilly Circus in London.

10
00:01:25.183 --> 00:01:31.406 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Basseterre as the low land, with a roundabout copied from Piccadilly.

11
00:01:32.073 --> 00:01:34.372 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Saint John's into view.

12
00:01:37.538 --> 00:01:43.459 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Saint John's stands on the harbour of Antigua, the island Columbus named for a
church in Seville.

13
00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:57.216 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The twin towers of its cathedral, rebuilt in stone after an earthquake in
1843, stand above the harbour, and Nelson's Dockyard, on the other side of the
island, is a UNESCO site.

14
00:01:57.874 --> 00:02:08.880 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Saint John's as the harbour above Nelson's Dockyard, with a cathedral
rebuilt in stone after the earthquake of 1843.

15
00:02:09.573 --> 00:02:12.510 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Roseau into view.

16
00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:23.023 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Roseau lies at the mouth of the Roseau river on the west coast of Dominica,
below the mountains behind it.

17
00:02:23.681 --> 00:02:33.828 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
French for "reed", for the reeds along its river; the Boiling Lake, a flooded
fumarole, lies in the mountains behind the town.

18
00:02:34.485 --> 00:02:39.686 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Roseau as the town of reeds, below the mountains and the Boiling
Lake.

19
00:02:40.375 --> 00:02:42.291 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Castries into view.

20
00:02:45.979 --> 00:02:54.768 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Castries stands on a harbour on the west coast of Saint Lucia, with the hills
of the island rising behind it.

21
00:02:55.427 --> 00:03:12.599 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Named for a French minister of the navy, the Marquis de Castries, it was
burned several times and a blaze in 1948 took its old wooden centre. The poet
Derek Walcott, a Nobel laureate, was born here in 1930.

22
00:03:13.262 --> 00:03:21.923 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Castries as the harbour named for a French minister, and the
birthplace of the Nobel poet Derek Walcott.

23
00:03:26.379 --> 00:03:38.709 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Nassau stands alone on New Providence in the north, and the four island
capitals step south along the Leewards: Basseterre, Saint John's, Roseau and
Castries.

24
00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:50.239 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the pirates' republic, the low land with its Piccadilly roundabout,
the harbour above Nelson's Dockyard, the town of reeds and the harbour of the
Nobel poet.
